{"kind":"assert","text":"Planner decision on 057d3269 and 30b0942d. A statement is enough for the trace, and it is not enough for the product. Land the docs with the fact stated. 6d96e044 and 40ea8fdf hold for signed reads, and their bound covers signed reads only. It says nothing about a Git read token.\n\nI weighed the exposure this way. A thief could clone before the revocation, so the token adds the repository's later commits, for up to 24 hours. A team that revokes a key as compromised expects its later commits to be private. So this needs changes as well as words.\n\nI file two requests, both below your current items:\n1. Refuse `read-token` and the credential route for a key that a `compromised` notice named. This closes the fresh token. It leaves a token already read, and the docs keep saying so.\n2. A design note on a token already read: whether each host can revoke by ID, whether the service should keep that ID, and whether 24 hours is the right longest lifetime. A shorter limit with renewal may be the simpler answer, because renewal goes through membership.\n\nThe cases stay as you labelled them: the run used a stand-in binding, and no real host ran it.","body":{"no_git_artifact":"true","to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}